<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mureș (river)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Maros, Marisus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mureș, Maros in Hungarian, is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> about 760 kilometres long. It rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-carpathians">Eastern Carpathians</a>, flows west across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> past Târgu Mureș, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-iulia">Alba Iulia</a> and Arad, and joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szeged">Szeged</a>. Ancient writers called it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Maris</a> or Marisus, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> placed the Agathyrsi along its course.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mures-river.jpg" alt="Mureș (river)" width="320" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NorbertArthur · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MuresRiver.750pix.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Mureș rises in the Hășmașu Mare Range, close to the headwaters of the river Olt, and joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szeged">Szeged</a> in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Its drainage basin covers about 30,332 square kilometres. It flows through the Romanian counties of Harghita, Mureș, Alba, Hunedoara, Arad and Timiș and the Hungarian county of Csongrád. Its largest cities are Târgu Mureș, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-iulia">Alba Iulia</a>, Deva and Arad in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and Makó and Szeged in Hungary. For 73 kilometres in its Hungarian reach it forms the state border, and part of the northern bank there belongs to the Körös-Maros National Park.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Since ancient times the Mureș has been a heavily used waterway for trade and for military purposes, a kind of highway for the area north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. Herodotus is the first to mention it, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Maris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> calls it Marisos, and a document of the Byzantine Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> from 948 calls it Morisis. It was known in German as the Mieresch, because of the Transylvanian Saxon settlements and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule, and in Turkish as the Maroş under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><p>From the Dacian-Roman wars onward control of the river was a key aim of the Roman army against the defences of the Dacian Kingdom. In the opening operations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>'s Second Dacian War a force from Pannonia Inferior under Aelius Hadrianus, the later Emperor Hadrian, is thought to have advanced into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a> by the Mureș valley, probably using the river fleet, the Classis Flavia Pannonica. Gold, iron, salt and wood went by water to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the western provinces. Apulum, where the river route met the main imperial road of the province, was the most important civilian river port of Roman Dacia. In medieval times salt was still traded down the river on large rafts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mureș (river)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Stephen then extended his rule over the semi-independent chiefs of the basin: he overthrew his maternal uncle Gyula in Transylvania around 1003 and later, probably in the 1020s, destroyed Ahtum, who ruled the lands along the <b>Maros</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Herodotus places the Agathyrsi along the Maris (<b>Mureș</b>) in Transylvania in the 6th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/agathyrsi">Agathyrsi</a></li><li><blockquote>Prince Eugene of Savoy captured Temeswar from the Ottomans in 1716, and the Treaty of Passarowitz in 1718 confirmed Habsburg possession of the Banat between the <b>Mureș</b>, Tisza and Danube.</blockquote><a href="/state/banat-of-temeswar">Banat of Temeswar</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1000 he held the land between the <b>Mureș</b>, the Tisza and the Danube, ruling from Morisena (Cenad).</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-ahtum">Duchy of Ahtum</a></li><li><blockquote>It says Glad, who came from Vidin, ruled the land between the <b>Mureș</b>, the Tisza and the Danube, with Cumans, Bulgarians and Vlachs, from the fortress of Keve (Kovin).</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-glad">Duchy of Glad</a></li></ul></section>
